+  "storyCampaign": {
+    "id": "the-open-ledger",
+    "title": "The Open Ledger",
+    "language": "en",
+    "intro": "Lorn's council has announced that the evacuation is complete. Mara Rook can still hear people behind the gate. The dead at the wheel are obeying an old instruction, and eleven bells repeat it across the city. A twelfth bell has no name. Enter the cordon, find the people the ledger left out, and decide what the city should remember.",
+    "acts": [
+      {
+        "title": "A way inside",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "The first task is small enough to say aloud: open a door and bring someone through it. The gate will lead to a ward, a workshop and a list. The order in which we reach them matters."
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The work of the living",
+        "speaker": "Tamsin Reed",
+        "text": "The city still works. Ovens burn, clocks turn and pumps empty the river into the streets. Their operators are dead. Somewhere a command is being copied faster than we can break it."
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "Everyone has a name",
+        "speaker": "Sable Quill",
+        "text": "The council's final bell does not command the dead to rise. It tells them whom to ignore. We have followed the order to its source. Now we must choose what will replace it."
+      }
+    ],
+    "beats": [
+      {
+        "title": "The hinge",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "The gate stops repeating its lock order. Beneath the wheel we find a brass plate: EVACUATION COMPLETE. Fresh chalk names cover the wall below it. Someone inside the cordon has been keeping a different account.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Bring out the families",
+            "text": "Make room for the people waiting at the triage passage.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Copy the chalk names",
+            "text": "Take a witness list through the old registry gate.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Cut the alarm wire",
+            "text": "Break the signal that keeps the workshops sealed.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "Three addresses",
+        "speaker": "Iona Vale",
+        "text": "A porter gives us three addresses and only one unbroken handcart. One holds patients, one holds their admission slips, and one holds the mechanism that marks the slips refused. We cannot be everywhere at once.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Take the cart to the ward",
+            "text": "Carry blankets and drinking water toward the infirmary.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Recover the admission slips",
+            "text": "Keep proof of who was turned away.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Stop the refusal machine",
+            "text": "Remove the seal before more doors obey it.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The red door",
+        "speaker": "Bram Voss",
+        "text": "The door is painted red on both sides. The people behind it were told that red meant safety; the watch were told that it meant infection. Bram takes the handle off and carries it with him. There will be no second locking.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Escort the people inside",
+            "text": "Give the slowest walkers a protected route.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Find the watch order",
+            "text": "Read the original instruction rather than another copy.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Break the locking gear",
+            "text": "Make the next red door impossible to close.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The measure",
+        "speaker": "Sable Quill",
+        "text": "The council called its sorting device a diagnostic instrument. Its scales have no weight marks, only columns for accepted and unlisted. The glass is cracked from the inside. Someone tried to make the result come out differently.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Treat the unlisted",
+            "text": "Take the rejected patients somewhere a form cannot stop them.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Keep the broken measure",
+            "text": "Carry the instrument as evidence of what it was built to do.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Destroy the stencil",
+            "text": "Stop the workshops making another identical scale.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "No one left outside",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "The next cart reaches the gate without an admission stamp. Nothing terrible happens. A child asks why everyone looks surprised. Beyond the wall, the bells begin the next shift. We have opened one door; the city can still close a thousand.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Shelter the first convoy",
+            "text": "Prepare a quiet place for the next group to arrive.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Deliver the witness list",
+            "text": "Let the outer watch see whose names were missing.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Follow the shift signal",
+            "text": "Trace the order back through the working machinery.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The bread line",
+        "speaker": "Hux Bell",
+        "text": "The ovens are full, but the queue is empty. Hux finds a week's wages tied into a baker's apron. The flour has been counted as fuel. He remembers working hungry beside this furnace, when the bells still called it a privilege.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Send the flour to the ward",
+            "text": "Turn a counted resource into something people can eat.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Record the unpaid names",
+            "text": "Carry the workers' accounts out of the furnace hall.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Pull the coal chain",
+            "text": "Stop the next shift before the furnace can demand it.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "Unpaid hours",
+        "speaker": "Tamsin Reed",
+        "text": "Every clock has kept the same minute. Each time its hand reaches the next mark, a ratchet pulls it back. Tamsin could repair the ratchet with her eyes closed. Instead she asks who benefits from a shift that cannot end.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Give the crews a rest",
+            "text": "Move the trapped workers toward the infirmary courtyard.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Take the time sheets",
+            "text": "Preserve the hours that the clocks refused to finish.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Remove the ratchet",
+            "text": "Let the machines reach tomorrow without another command.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The flooded register",
+        "speaker": "Vale Ash",
+        "text": "The post bag is too heavy to lift until the water drains from it. Each letter begins with the same apology for being late. Vale finds one addressed to a person who has been helping us since the gate. Nobody has told them the letter arrived.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Ferry the waiting families",
+            "text": "Use the chapel steps as a dry gathering place.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Deliver every letter",
+            "text": "Bring the bag to the registry and find its living addresses.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Open the flood controls",
+            "text": "Cut the command that keeps the streets under water.",
+            "arenaId": "ossuary-works"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "A reason to sing",
+        "speaker": "Sable Quill",
+        "text": "The choir's score has no words. Its rests are marked with tiny names, crossed out one by one. When Sable reads a name into the silence, someone on the far side of the room answers. The bells hesitate for a single beat.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Keep a quiet room",
+            "text": "Make space for the voices that are still frightened to answer.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Carry the marked score",
+            "text": "Keep the erased names beside the witnesses who know them.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Disconnect the amplifier",
+            "text": "Stop the order being made louder than a human voice.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The city answers",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "Three districts answer the radio without giving a registration number. The council replies that this is impossible: its ledger has no empty beds, no unpaid hours and no undelivered post. Then a second voice whispers a question beneath the official broadcast. How many names have we found?",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Keep the convoys moving",
+            "text": "Let the answer be the people arriving outside the cordon.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Bring the ledger to council",
+            "text": "Place the witnesses beside the account that erased them.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Sever the broadcast line",
+            "text": "Find the machinery that keeps speaking over the second voice.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "One blank column",
+        "speaker": "Iona Vale",
+        "text": "The blank column is not a space for people whose names were lost. It is a place for people the council decided not to name. Iona reads the small print twice. There was always room in the infirmary; the empty beds were being held for better entries.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Give the beds to the living",
+            "text": "Finish the evacuation before arguing with another form.",
+            "arenaId": "triage-yard"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Trace every blank entry",
+            "text": "Ask each witness who should have occupied the missing line.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Break the reservation seals",
+            "text": "Prevent the blank column being used again.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The procession",
+        "speaker": "Bram Voss",
+        "text": "The procession carries empty boxes beneath the city banners. Bram recognizes the step: he once marched it at the head of an evacuation. Nobody is leading this one. The order has outlived everyone who could remember where it was meant to go.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Build a living corridor",
+            "text": "Hold a route for people who can choose their destination.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Identify the bearers",
+            "text": "Give the procession back the names its banners concealed.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Lower the city standard",
+            "text": "End the march instead of finding another leader for it.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "A bell without a rope",
+        "speaker": "Tamsin Reed",
+        "text": "The twelfth bell has no rope and no clapper. It rings when the other eleven agree that somebody does not count. Tamsin lays a hand against its housing and feels the metal hesitate. A machine can repeat an order. It cannot decide whom we owe an answer.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Protect the last carriers",
+            "text": "Make sure the witnesses reach the end of the route alive.",
+            "arenaId": "sunken-chapel"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Read the missing names",
+            "text": "Carry the second account into the council chamber.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Remove the governor",
+            "text": "Keep the bells from being forced into agreement again.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The last order",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "The council offers to open the cordon if we certify its original evacuation as complete. Nobody has to change a name; we only have to stop counting. Mara leaves the form unsigned. The last order is already coming down the wire. Choose what we will hold when it arrives.",
+        "choices": [
+          {
+            "title": "Hold the evacuation corridor",
+            "text": "Put the people coming home before any signature.",
+            "arenaId": "gatehouse"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Answer with the complete list",
+            "text": "Bring every recovered name into the final hearing.",
+            "arenaId": "crown-crypt"
+          },
+          {
+            "title": "Refuse the command",
+            "text": "Disconnect the authority that can issue another order.",
+            "arenaId": "bell-foundry"
+          }
+        ]
+      },
+      {
+        "title": "The account remains open",
+        "speaker": "Mara Rook",
+        "text": "The last order ends before its final word. Across Lorn, doors remain open without waiting for another instruction. We cannot bring back everyone the city lost. We can decide what it means to keep their place.",
+        "choices": []
+      }
+    ],
+    "endings": [
+      {
+        "id": "shelter",
+        "title": "A road stays open",
+        "speaker": "Iona Vale",
+        "text": "The ward runs out of forms before it runs out of beds. Iona uses the clean backs of the pages to write directions to the gate. Every convoy leaves room for somebody who walks slowly. On the first morning without an order, Mara asks whether they should close the cordon. Nobody can think of a reason. The city will remember the missing by making space for whoever arrives next."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "witness",
+        "title": "Names answered",
+        "speaker": "Vale Ash",
+        "text": "They put two ledgers on the council table. One says the evacuation was complete. The other contains handwriting from every district: a cook's mark, a child's drawing, names somebody remembered while carrying a stretcher. Vale leaves the last page blank and places a pencil beside it. Every evening the witnesses read what has been added. The twelfth bell is quiet while somebody is answering."
+      },
+      {
+        "id": "defiance",
+        "title": "No order after this",
+        "speaker": "Tamsin Reed",
+        "text": "Tamsin removes the governor without replacing it. The eleven bells can sound the hours, but none can command a door to close. Hux asks how the next shift will know when to begin. People can ask each other, she says. Beyond the foundry, one surviving wire trembles against the stones. They leave it there as a reminder: a city should never be so well ordered that nobody can refuse it."
+      }
+    ],
+    "choiceSeconds": 45
+  },
